k this first we  see  bishop to b7 so both of the players  eyeing down on  these light squares you can see this  pawn here on e4  starting to stand out is a square that  both sides may want to attack or defend  the bishop  here on b7 attacking as well as the  knight here on  f6 white immediately comes up and plays  d3 to defend this  and jeffrey comes down to c4 and attacks  the pawn here  now there is an exchange that does open  up for the queen to  defend this but you can see now that the  queen comes down there's a queen  bishop and knight all attacking this  pawn here  on e4  now my assumption is that alejandro  played this  opening because the move knight to d2 is  pretty tricky  and if black's not prepared for this can  fall into  a lot of mistakes you can already see  that the bishop  knights and queen here are all attacking  this pawn on e4 and it's overloading  the square and white can't defend it  with just the queen and even the knight  here on d2 that's only two pieces but  this knight is attacking the queen  black could feel overconfident and  actually take with the bishop here on e4  saying yeah i'm attacking your queen so  even if you take my queen i can just  take your queen right here  that's not necessarily a bad thing and  black was able to capture this material  here on e4  but instead white can take here on e4  and after the queen takes a queen takes  the knight takes here on e4  it looks like black came up in the  exchange  but then knight two d2 and this is where  black loses because if you start to  analyze where does black really go from  here well  if the knight moves then you can see the  bishop is going to just take  the rook here on a8 so that's not going  to work out well  if they tried to defend this pawn to  d5 okay we'll just knight takes here if  the pawn takes bishop recaptures and the  rook is going to fall  so that's not going to work out well so  at the end of the day  although black does have a ton of  material that's actually 