  my bishop here on e4 this would actually  be  a big mistake because the bishop can  take here  on d4 so after bishop takes  then the pawn recaptures and all of a  sudden this  knight is pinned to the queen here on  c2 and anytime a piece  is pinned down to a more powerful piece  like a queen  or a king you always want to attack that  if you can attack it with a pawn  that is priority numero uno so that  would be exactly what jeffrey would do  in this spot he would play pawn  to b5 in the night would  fall so sometimes that is pretty tricky  to  think ahead as far as oh wow if i  actually take with my bishop  then they can take and it's going to  create that  pin here but being a top class player  that's exactly what alejandro looks at  and he  correctly takes with his queen here on  e4  knight to c5 attacking the queen queen  comes back to e3  knight takes here on b3  and then we have knight coming back here  taking on  uh b3 but that also leaves this  knight here on a c4 so lots of  exchanging on the board here night now  coming here to d4 this is an outpost but  obviously can take with the bishop if  jeffrey wants to pawn down here to b5  so both solidifying this is an outpost  not generally the piece that you're  having an outpost with your pawn it's  usually going to be a bishop or  a knight but either way it's still  protected here  on c4 then we see the bishop come up  here to  d5 attacking the rook so the rook comes  over here attacks this bishop  here on a3 bishop comes down b3 attacks  the rook  rook retreats to a6 knight down to  c2 and as we've kind of talked about in  the past once your opponent starts to  retreat their knights  that's an opportunity to start to attack  jeffrey starts to push forward with his  pawn here on  e6 we see the rook swing over here to e6  doubling up the queen  and the rook and then queen to a7 just  says hey if you want to exchange that's  fine and this really comes into the  endgame of  what jeffrey feels like he is going to  do the best at  and you can see that from white's  perspective  he has two minor pieces and on the other  side  in exchange for those two minor pieces  you're really looking at a rook  and two pawns so that feels like a win  for jeffrey so that's why he decided to  go and exchange off the queens  both sides continue to exchange off  material  bishop takes here on d6 also leaves the  pawn here on  c3 vulnerable bishop takes here on c3  rook to b1 it's under attack here rook 2  d8 attacking the bishop  bishop comes back so white does have the  bishop pair  but at the end game two rooks together  are definitely a powerful powerful  weapon  as well as having the bishop as well  rook over here to d7 getting them both  on this open file on the center of the  board definitely a good strategy  knight to a3 and then just comes down b4  great outpost for the bishop here  on c3 knight 2  c4 and then a rook over here to a7  king to f1 trying to get involved into  the game once the queens come off the  board a little bit safer to get the  kings towards the center of the board it  becomes a very powerful piece  and an attack here rook down to a1 says  yeah  i'm fine exchanging material here bishop  takes on a one  king to e2 uh bishop here to c3 so i  would give jeffrey a slight advantage i  do think the rook  is a very very powerful piece in this  setup  but it's going to be very difficult for  jeffrey to find  a victory here knight 2  e5 starting to attack this  f7 square at some point then we have  rook  2 a8 and then king  2 d3 and this is the decisive move  in the game here because if we look at  this position right here  knight 2 e5 this is  pretty dangerous for white and jeffrey  recognizes some of the threats that he  has  here because you always want to look at  what's protecting the material  and this knight right here is protected  by the bishop on f4  and black can actually play pawn to g5  as a deflection move and that's going to  force this bishop away from protecting  the knight  now in this specific position white can  just take  the pawn on g5 and then it's attacking  the rook here on d8 it has to move so  while the knight's no longer protected  the knight is going to be fine because  the rook has to move but  jeffrey decided to go ahead and play  rook to a8 he  saw the potential threat of g5 but he  had to get the rook out of the way  but then king 2 d3 is a massive error  really white needed to play knight to  d3 probably the only saving move that he  has because  after knight to d3 now g5  and in this spot alejandro ramirez  decided to resign because there's just  no way to  continue in this spot if he plays knight  takes on f7 just says okay  something's gonna fall here then just  take the bishop maybe the knight comes  back that's fine  rook to d8 check but this in game  jeffrey should easily win and  if the bishop moves that's fine then  you're just going to see the bishop take  here on e5  so at the end of the day jeffrey wins so  kudos to him unfortunately for him  wesley so ended up  drawing against nakamura  and so wesley so is the champion  definitely needed  a loss from wesley so for a tiebreaker  thought that would have been truly  awesome to see if the teenager could  have  pulled it out now those two did actually  play that was the only loss that jeffrey  had was playing wesley so  if he could have drawn that one then we  also would have had a tiebreaker at the  very end with those two but  hopefully everyone enjoyed this  tournament always nice to see  people in the u.s chess championship  especially that the younger players  do extremely well hopefully they can do  that at the  the greater international stage as well  but this is always a fun tournament for  me to analyze hopefully enjoyed it as  well  again thanks for supporting the channel  if you haven't already please subscribe  also would be so kind of you if you hit  that like button  to let me know that you like the video  and the series that we did  on the us chess championship but thanks  again for watching  i'll see you guys in the next video

